$string['help_b_configuration'] = '<p>Enable advanced grading on a per-activity basis. Open the BigBlueButton activity\'s edit form, expand the <strong>Advanced grading</strong> section, and set:</p>
<dl>
<dt>Grading method</dt>
<dd>One of <em>None</em>, <em>Rubric</em>, or <em>Marking guide</em>. The two non-None options enable the rubric / guide editor under Advanced grading in the activity\'s secondary navigation. Selecting <em>None</em> leaves the activity using its default numeric grade and skips this plugin entirely.</dd>
<dt>Score mode</dt>
<dd><em>Composite</em> pushes a single overall score to the gradebook. <em>Analytic</em> creates one grade item per analytic group declared by the template (e.g. one per Community of Inquiry presence) so the development of each dimension can be tracked separately. Analytic falls back to composite when the chosen rubric doesn\'t expose any groups.</dd>
<dt>Send rubric score to the gradebook</dt>
<dd>When on, the rubric\'s final score is the gradebook value for the user. Off lets you grade with the rubric for feedback purposes without affecting the gradebook.</dd>
</dl>
<p>The activity must have a numeric maximum grade set before advanced grading can be enabled - the rubric needs a target scale to map into. After saving the activity, define the rubric (or import a template - see below) via <strong>Advanced grading</strong> in the activity\'s secondary navigation, then optionally configure metric mappings.</p>';
$string['help_b_grading_interface'] = '<p>The <strong>Participation grading</strong> entry in the activity\'s secondary navigation lists every enrolled student with their current grade status. Clicking <em>Grade</em> opens the per-user grading page, which has three regions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Engagement evidence</strong>: a per-criterion summary of the BigBlueButton metrics the activity captured for this user - attendance time, microphone time, chat messages, hand-raises, poll votes, emoji reactions - together with any suggested rubric levels derived from the metric mapping (see below).</li>
<li><strong>Rubric or marking guide</strong>: the standard Moodle grading form for the chosen method. Filling it in produces a score that is recorded against the user and (optionally) pushed to the gradebook.</li>
<li><strong>Recording annotations</strong>: an own-player video of the BigBlueButton recording with a timeline strip below it. Each existing comment shows as a coloured marker on the timeline; hovering reveals a tooltip with the category, timestamp, and preview text. Clicking a marker opens a feedback callout right over the player - a circle for video feedback (with an expand toggle for screencast detail), a squircle for audio or text. The teacher can add timestamped comments using the editor below the player, save reusable snippets to a personal or course library, and play back any comment without scrolling away from the recording.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each comment carries a category: <em>General</em>, <em>Praise</em>, <em>Correction</em>, <em>Suggestion</em>, or <em>Question</em>. The categories drive the marker colour and the colour of the badge on the comment card so the timeline is at-a-glance scannable.</p>';
$string['help_b_groups'] = '<p>The plugin doesn\'t add its own Moodle-group filter on the grading list - the enrolled-students roll is shown unfiltered. Group-aware filtering is therefore handled at the layers around it:</p>
<ul>
<li>The <strong>BigBlueButton activity\'s own group mode</strong> (set in the activity edit form) controls which users see each other in the meeting and which recordings each group can access. When set to <em>Separate groups</em>, BigBlueButton hides cross-group recordings; our recording picker on the annotation overlay reflects that scope.</li>
<li>Engagement evidence is captured <strong>per individual user</strong>, regardless of group. Two students from different groups who attended the same meeting both have their own metric rows; the grading list shows them both alongside everyone else.</li>
<li>If the activity uses Visible groups or Separate groups, teachers can still use the standard course-level group selector to narrow the participants visible to them - that selector is provided by Moodle\'s page header, not by this plugin.</li>
<li>Annotation comments are scoped per <em>recording × student</em>, so a comment on student A in group 1 is invisible to student B in group 2 by construction, even when they share a recording.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your grading workflow needs a stronger group filter (e.g. a tutor only sees their tutorial group on the participation list), use Moodle\'s standard group-mode + tutor role at the course level.</p>';
$string['help_b_metric_mapping'] = '<p>For each rubric criterion you can declare a BigBlueButton engagement metric whose value should suggest a rubric level. The mapping lives under <strong>Metric mappings</strong> in the activity\'s secondary navigation and is structured as:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Criterion</strong> - one of the rubric or guide criteria defined for the activity.</li>
<li><strong>Metric</strong> - one of: <em>Attendance time</em>, <em>Mic talk time</em>, <em>Chat messages</em>, <em>Raise hand</em>, <em>Poll votes</em>, <em>Emoji reactions</em>, or <em>Composite engagement score</em>. <em>None</em> disables the suggestion for that criterion.</li>
<li><strong>Thresholds (JSON)</strong> - a JSON object mapping <em>level id</em> (or the level score) to the minimum metric value that justifies that level. Example for a time-based metric (seconds): <code>{"3":600,"2":240,"1":60,"0":0}</code> means a student needs &gt;=10 minutes of talk time for level 3, &gt;=4 minutes for level 2, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Weight</strong> - the contribution of this metric to the composite engagement score for the activity. The defaults come from the site-level <em>Default weight</em> settings under Site administration so a new mapping inherits a sensible starting point.</li>
</ul>
<p>The plugin then computes a per-criterion suggested level when you grade a user. The suggestion is advisory only - it appears as a hint above the rubric and never auto-applies. The teacher\'s judgement on the rubric overrides every suggestion.</p>
<p>The <em>Composite engagement score</em> is a weighted blend of the underlying metrics, normalised so a relatively engaged user scores higher than a passive one. It\'s useful when you want a single criterion that captures \"participated meaningfully\" without committing to a specific channel.</p>';
$string['help_b_multiple_sessions'] = '<p>A BigBlueButton activity is a long-lived room; one activity can host many sessions across a term. The plugin treats <strong>sessions and recordings as accumulating</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Engagement metrics accumulate</strong>. The BigBlueButton broker calls back to Moodle at the end of each meeting; our hook (<code>broker_meeting_events_addons::process_action</code>) reads the per-attendee summary and merges it into the evidence snapshot for that user. A student who joins three sessions has their talk time, chat count, etc. summed across all three.</li>
<li><strong>Recordings are per session</strong>. Each session that was recorded persists as its own recording, listed by BigBlueButton in the order they were made. The annotation overlay shows a recording picker when more than one recording exists; switching the picker reloads the overlay against the selected recording, keeping its own comment list and timeline markers.</li>
<li><strong>Comments are scoped per recording × student</strong>. A comment you write on session 2\'s recording for Alice is independent of any comment on session 1\'s recording for the same student. This lets you grade the development of a student across the term without one session\'s feedback contaminating another\'s.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence is frozen at grading time</strong>. When you submit a rubric grade we snapshot the engagement metric totals at that moment into the grade row\'s <code>evidence</code> field. Later sessions add to the live metrics for future grading, but the historical grade keeps the metrics that justified it.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a recording is deleted in BigBlueButton it disappears from the recording picker, but any annotations attached to it remain in the database (deleting upstream recordings is not something Moodle is told about). You can review and remove them through the grading page.</p>';
$string['help_b_participation'] = '<p>The plugin is technically a <em>sub-plugin</em> of mod_bigbluebuttonbn (its component name is <code>bbbext_advgrd</code>) and registers itself as the owner of a grading area named <strong>BigBlueButton participation</strong>. That gives it three things the standard activity grade doesn\'t have on its own:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Its own rubric or marking guide</strong>, defined and edited via Moodle\'s standard grading editor under the activity\'s Advanced grading entry. Because the area is owned by us, it doesn\'t interfere with any other grade items on the activity.</li>
<li><strong>Composite or analytic gradebook items</strong>. In composite mode the rubric score is sent as a single overall grade. In analytic mode, each template group (e.g. cognitive presence, social presence) becomes its own grade item, so the gradebook shows the development of each dimension separately. Analytic mode is set at the activity level and falls back to composite when the rubric exposes no analytic groups.</li>
<li><strong>Frozen evidence snapshots</strong>. Each saved grade carries a JSON snapshot of the BigBlueButton metrics that supported it. The snapshot is shown on the per-user grading page and is preserved across later sessions so the grade\'s justification doesn\'t silently change as new sessions land.</li>
</ol>
<p>The grading capability (<code>bbbext/advgrd:grade</code>) is granted to teachers and editing-teachers by default. Configuration of the rubric / mappings is gated separately by <code>bbbext/advgrd:manage</code>, which by default is editing-teacher and manager only. Students get <code>bbbext/advgrd:viewownreport</code> so they can see their own grade and feedback comments via Moodle\'s standard <em>View feedback</em> route (a custom student-side view is also exposed through unified_grader when installed).</p>
<h5 class="mt-3">A note on <em>Validate completion</em></h5>
<p>You\'ll see a <strong>Validate completion</strong> entry in the same secondary navigation as our nodes. That link belongs to <code>mod_bigbluebuttonbn</code> itself (not this plugin) and only appears when the site has the <em>Register live sessions</em> admin setting enabled and the user can edit the activity. Clicking it asks the BigBlueButton server for the latest per-user attendance summary and recomputes Moodle\'s <strong>activity completion</strong> state - it\'s useful when the end-of-meeting webhook was missed and completion didn\'t tick over for users who were actually present. It doesn\'t change anything in this plugin\'s grading state; rubric grades and frozen evidence snapshots are independent of activity completion. For full details consult the BigBlueButton activity documentation.</p>';
$string['help_b_submissions'] = '<p>BigBlueButton activities don\'t have a file or text submission - the user\'s <strong>participation in a meeting is the submission</strong>. The plugin treats a user as having submitted when either of the following is true:</p>
<ul>
<li>BigBlueButton recorded a <code>JOIN</code> event for them on this activity (the user joined at least one meeting), or</li>
<li>The end-of-meeting broker has produced an <code>EVENT_SUMMARY</code> for them (the analytics callback path - which fires once per meeting when the <em>Register live sessions</em> admin setting is on).</li>
</ul>
<p>This OR is deliberate: not every BigBlueButton site has the analytics callback enabled, so we fall back to the join log as a baseline indicator of attendance. A user with neither is shown as <em>No submission</em> on the participation list.</p>
<p>For the grade itself, the rubric / marking guide is the source of truth. The user is <em>graded</em> when a row exists in our grade table for them; until then they\'re <em>submitted, not graded</em> (or <em>no submission</em> if they were never present). The status drives the colour-coded badges on the participation list.</p>';
$string['help_b_templates'] = '<p>Under <strong>Import template</strong> in the activity\'s secondary navigation, the plugin ships three research-anchored starter rubrics. Each is a complete rubric <em>and</em> a complete marking guide derived from the same criteria; importing populates both forms and seeds metric mappings for the criteria that can be auto-linked to a BigBlueButton signal.</p>
<dl>
<dt>Community of Inquiry</dt>
<dd>Four criteria a teacher can observe in a single live session: <em>triggering inquiry</em> and <em>connecting ideas</em> (cognitive presence), and <em>open communication</em> and <em>group cohesion</em> (social presence). Teaching presence is deliberately omitted from the starter - import it manually if students peer-lead the session. Citation: Garrison, Anderson &amp; Archer (2000); revised for single-session use after Akyol &amp; Garrison (2008). The mappable criteria connect to mic talk time (open communication) and chat messages (connecting ideas).</dd>
<dt>Quantity + Quality</dt>
<dd>Splits participation into a <em>quantity</em> axis (frequency, sustained presence) wired to attendance + chat + talk metrics, and a <em>quality</em> axis (depth of contribution, active listening) that stays judgement-only because raw volume cannot tell you whether a contribution was substantive. Citation: Bean &amp; Peterson (1998); Armstrong &amp; Boud (1983).</dd>
<dt>Inclusive multi-modal</dt>
<dd>One criterion per modality - <em>oral contribution</em> (mic time), <em>written contribution</em> (chat messages), <em>responsive engagement</em> (raise hand + poll + emoji), <em>group work</em> (judgement only, set after the breakout) - so a student strong on one modality isn\'t penalised for being silent on another. Designed to acknowledge that vocal participation alone is a poor proxy for engagement. Citation: Shi &amp; Tan (2020); Crosthwaite, Bailey &amp; Meeker (2015); Tani (2008); O\'Connor et al. (2017).</dd>
</dl>
<p>The connection to BigBlueButton analytic data flows through metric mapping (see next section): each template tags which of its criteria can carry a metric-derived suggestion, and the import step pre-fills those mappings with sensible thresholds drawn from the published literature. After import, every aspect of the rubric remains editable via the standard Moodle rubric editor.</p>
<p>If you don\'t want the bundled templates available, the <em>Ship starter templates</em> site setting under <em>Site administration</em> &gt; <em>Plugins</em> &gt; <em>BBB Advanced Grading</em> hides the Import template entry.</p>';
